The '''2022 United States Senate election in Idaho''' was held on November 8, 2022, to elect a member of the [[United States Senate]] to represent the [[U.S. State|state]] of [[Idaho]]. Incumbent [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] Senator [[Mike Crapo]] was first elected in [[1998 United States Senate election in Idaho|1998]] and ran for re-election to a fifth term in office.<ref name="cnn">{{Cite web|author=Alex Rogers, Manu Raju and Ted Barrett|title=Retirements shake up 2022 map as Republican senators eye exits|url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/01/26/politics/retirements-republicans-2022/index.html|access-date=2021-02-15|website=CNN|date=January 27, 2021 }}</ref><ref name="idahostatesman.com">{{cite web | last=Brizee | first=Alex | title=Idaho's Mike Crapo will run for a fifth U.S. Senate term in 2022 midterm elections | website=Idaho Statesman | date=5 January 2022 | url=https://www.idahostatesman.com/news/politics-government/state-politics/article257061642.html | access-date=10 April 2023}}</ref> Primary elections were held on May 17, 2022. Crapo easily won renomination, while former [[Idaho House of Representatives]] candidate David Roth won the Democratic primary with 57.8% of the vote. Crapo ultimately won the election, but this was the lowest share of the vote he had ever received for this seat, partially because of an independent candidate, Scott Cleveland, taking away some of his votes.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Idaho Republican U.S. Sen. Crapo wins fifth term |language=en-US |newspaper=Washington Post |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/idaho-republican-sen-crapo-goes-for-5th-term/2022/11/08/a88e94f8-5f65-11ed-a131-e900e4a6336b_story.html |access-date=2022-11-09 |issn=0190-8286}}</ref>

The election resulted in both the worst performance by a Republican, and the best performance by a Democrat in the [[Class 3 Senator|Class 3 seat]] since [[1992 United States Senate election in Idaho|1992]]. It was also the best performance by a [[Third party (U.S. politics)|third-party]] or [[Independent politician|independent]] candidate for a Senate seat in Idaho since [[1926 United States Senate elections#Idaho|1926]].

==Republican primary==
===Candidates===

====Nominee====
*[[Mike Crapo]], incumbent U.S. senator<ref name=cnn/><ref name="idahostatesman.com"/>

====Eliminated in primary====
*Brenda Bourn<ref>{{cite web |title=BOURN, BRENDA - Candidate overview |website=www.fec.gov|url=https://www.fec.gov/data/candidate/S2ID00160/?cycle=2022&election_full=true |access-date=19 February 2022}}</ref>
